Title: local CI skips cleanly when the repository has no workflow
Created: 2026-10-12
Author: Minh Ha-Duong

--- log ---
2026-10-12T08:04Z Minh Ha-Duong created

--- body ---
## Context

The shared `scripts/local-ci.sh` sources `<repo>/ci-local/common.sh` before it checks for `.github/workflows/CI.yml`. In repositories that intentionally have no CI, including `climate-finance-het` (see that project's decision 0321), it errors on the absent helper directory instead of reporting a clean skip. Keep the no-CI decision; make the generic runner distinguish “no workflow” from a broken local-CI setup.

## Relevant files
- `scripts/local-ci.sh` — workflow and helper preflight order.
- `tests/test_local_ci_no_workflow.py` — hermetic subprocess coverage.

## Actions
1. Detect a missing workflow before sourcing project helper files; print an explicit `SKIP` and return the documented no-workflow skip code 3.
2. If a workflow exists but `ci-local/common.sh` is missing, print a clear setup error and exit non-zero.
3. Add regression tests for both cases.

## Test
- Build temporary git repositories in pytest; run the committed runner with no workflow and with a workflow but no helper directory.

## Invariants
- Existing CI repositories retain all current validation and runner behavior.
- A skip is labeled as a skip, never a pass.
- No CI workflow or local runner assets are added to projects that intentionally have no CI.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] No workflow: runner returns the documented no-workflow skip code 3 before probing `act`, `podman`, or `ci-local/`, and says no checks ran.
- [ ] Workflow present but helper absent: runner exits non-zero with a clear diagnostic.
- [ ] Regression tests cover both paths and pass.
- [ ] `make check-fast` and `make lint` pass.
